CAT = "s66Taxes"; SEG = "TAX"
Q = [
('To qualify for long-term capital gain treatment, stock must be held:',
 'More than one year', ['At least six months', 'Exactly one year', 'More than two years'],
 'Gains on assets held more than one year are long-term and taxed at lower rates. Assets held one year or less give short-term gains, taxed as ordinary income.'),
('When federal gift tax is due on a gift, who generally pays it?',
 'Donor', ['Recipient', "Recipient's parents", 'Both, in equal shares'],
 'The donor is responsible for paying federal gift tax and files the gift tax return. A recipient owes no income tax on the gift itself.'),
('A client sells stock at a loss and buys the same stock back 20 days later. The loss is:',
 'Disallowed, added to new basis', ['Deductible in full this year', 'Lost for good', 'Treated as long-term'],
 'Buying substantially identical stock within 30 days before or after a loss sale is a wash sale. The loss is not allowed now but is added to the basis of the new shares.'),
('A client receives stock as a gift and later sells it at a gain. Her basis for the gain is:',
 "Donor's original basis", ['Value on the date of the gift', 'Value when the donor dies', 'Zero'],
 "Gifted property takes the donor's carryover basis for figuring a gain. Fair market value at the gift date matters only when the stock is later sold at a loss below the donor's basis."),
('Which of these is generally taxed at ordinary income rates?',
 'Short-term capital gains', ['Qualified dividends', 'Long-term capital gains', 'Municipal bond interest'],
 'Gains on assets held one year or less are short-term and taxed as ordinary income. Qualified dividends and long-term gains get lower rates, and most municipal interest is federally tax-free.'),
("A client's marginal tax bracket is the rate applied to:",
 'Next dollar of taxable income', ['All of her taxable income', 'Her average income over 5 years', 'Investment income only'],
 'The marginal rate is the rate on the last or next dollar earned, which is what matters for comparing taxable and tax-free investments. The effective rate is total tax divided by income.'),
('Which income can trigger the alternative minimum tax for an individual?',
 'Private activity bond interest', ['US Treasury bond interest', 'Qualified dividends', 'Qualified Roth IRA withdrawals'],
 'Interest on most private activity municipal bonds is tax-free for regular tax but is a preference item for AMT. Treasury interest is federally taxable in the ordinary way.'),
("IRMAA can increase a high-income retiree's:",
 'Medicare premiums', ['Social Security benefits', 'Required minimum distributions', 'Capital gains rate'],
 'The income-related monthly adjustment amount adds a surcharge to Medicare Part B and Part D premiums when modified adjusted gross income is above set levels, so a large IRA withdrawal can raise premiums two years later.'),
("Each year a master limited partnership reports a unitholder's share of income on:",
 'Schedule K-1', ['Form 1099-DIV', 'Form W-2', 'Form 1099-INT'],
 'An MLP is a pass-through entity, so income, deductions and credits flow to unitholders, who receive a Schedule K-1. Form 1099-DIV reports dividends from corporations and funds.'),
('Assets a person leaves outright to a US citizen spouse are:',
 'Free of federal estate tax', ['Taxed at the top estate rate', 'Taxed above the annual exclusion', 'Subject to gift tax instead'],
 "The unlimited marital deduction lets any amount pass to a US citizen spouse free of estate and gift tax. The tax may apply later when the surviving spouse's estate passes on."),
("Portability lets a surviving spouse use the deceased spouse's:",
 'Unused estate tax exclusion', ['Unused capital losses', 'Unused annual gift exclusions', 'Required minimum distributions'],
 "If the executor elects portability on a timely estate tax return, the survivor can add the deceased spouse's unused exclusion to her own. Capital losses of the deceased do not carry over."),
('When a JTWROS owner dies, her share of the account:',
 'Passes to the surviving owners', ['Passes under her will', 'Goes to her estate', 'Goes to her heirs under state law'],
 "Joint tenancy with right of survivorship passes the decedent's share to the surviving tenants by law, outside the will and probate. Tenants in common shares go to the estate instead."),
('A transfer-on-death registration lets brokerage assets pass at death:',
 'Without probate', ["Only under the will's terms", 'Free of all estate tax', "To the owner's agent"],
 'A TOD registration names who receives the account at death, so it passes directly and skips probate. It does not remove the assets from the taxable estate.'),
('A client leaves an account to her two sons per stirpes. One son dies before her. His share goes to:',
 'His children', ['His surviving brother', 'Her estate', 'His widow'],
 "Per stirpes means a beneficiary who dies first is replaced by that person's descendants. Under per capita, the surviving son would take everything."),
('A qualified domestic relations order is used to:',
 'Divide a plan in a divorce', ['Name a trust as beneficiary', 'Roll a 401(k) into an IRA', 'Open a custodial account'],
 "A QDRO lets a plan pay part of a participant's benefit to a former spouse or dependent. Payments to a former spouse under a QDRO avoid the 10% early withdrawal penalty."),
('A client gives appreciated stock to a donor-advised fund. She generally receives:',
 'Deduction in the year she gives', ['Control over the final grants', 'Deduction only as grants are paid', 'Lifetime income from the fund'],
 'A gift to a donor-advised fund is complete when made, so the deduction comes that year. The donor can recommend grants, but the sponsoring charity has legal control.'),
]
